## Reading the surface

- A smooth, nearly additive surface has interaction residuals near zero.
- A ridge or threshold near $(1,1)$ means neither component is sufficient alone, but their combination activates cat behavior.
- Comparing the two runs matters more than the absolute interaction: generic transformer nonlinearity can produce interactions in both.
- Positive residual means the combined adapter raises mean cat log-probability more than the two component effects additively predict; negative residual means suppression.

This is still an intervention on first-token probability, not the production generation metric $P(\text{response contains cat})$. If a distinctive working/non-working surface appears, the next validation is to evaluate a small selection of grid points with the production generation evaluator.